<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Information retention from adapter to LLM-final layer (probe accuracy ratio <math id="A2.T7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 100). Retention <math id="A2.T7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{&gt;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{&gt;}</annotation></semantics></math>100% means the LLM amplifies the signal; <math id="A2.T7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{&lt;}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{&lt;}</annotation></semantics></math>100% means degradation. Non-aligned encoders show selective amplification (lexical recovers, speaker degrades); text-aligned encoders retain or slightly improve all types. LS = LibriSpeech, CD = CREMA-D, E50 = ESC-50, CO = COCO.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.8 </span>Lipschitz constants and Wasserstein distances</h3>

